  "output": "Spec ID: token-efficient-tool-responses-ticket01-spec\nRevision: r35-replacement\nFindings by ID: C1, C2, C3, C4\n\nAnchors below refer to `.scratch/token-efficient-tool-responses/spec.md`.\n\n### Findings\n\n**C1 — MAJOR — R6/T2 — High confidence.**  \n§2 R6, AC7/15/38/39 account for payloads, reservations, markers and tombstones, but do not bound empty namespace containers or deferred-retirement bookkeeping. Cheap false pass: retain those indefinitely while reported aggregate accounting stays correct. Many-session churn can therefore exhaust memory. **Close with:** a bounded retained-metadata invariant and a churn oracle demonstrating reclamation, including refused retirements and repeated retrieval, without cross-session eviction.\n\n**C2 — MAJOR — R8/T3–T5 — High confidence.**  \nR8 prohibits re-execution, but AC9/19 and T5 require no upstream invocation-count assertion. Cheap false pass: regenerate pages by re-executing a deterministic upstream; byte reconstruction still passes while side effects repeat. **Close with:** an observable oracle asserting exactly one original execution and zero additional executions across page retrieval, repeated retrieval and retrieval failures.\n\n**C3 — MAJOR — R1/T3–T4 — High confidence.**  \nAC1 permits an “at/below-ceiling” fixture; AC40 tests only ceiling+1. Cheap false pass: page results at `>= delivery_ceiling_bytes`, selecting below-ceiling AC1 fixtures. **Close with:** explicit default-boundary fixtures proving 8192 serialized bytes pass through unchanged and 8193 page. The normative inequality is clear; its equality oracle is missing.\n\n**C4 — MAJOR — R3/R5/T4–T5 — High confidence.**  \nAC19 checks previews on all four surfaces, while T3’s retrieval round-trip is explicitly call_tool-only and T5 does not parameterize producers. Cheap false pass: discovery/direct previews contain correctly shaped but unusable handles. **Close with:** retrieve each surface’s issued handle, reconstruct its independent golden bytes, and verify identical repeated-page delivery.\n\nThese are new findings; why earlier reviews missed them is unknown.\n\n### Per-requirement verdicts\n\nCandidate requirements:  \nR1 REVISE; R2 PASS; R3 REVISE; R4 PASS; R5 REVISE; R6 REVISE; R7 PASS; R8 REVISE; R9 PASS; R10 PASS; R11 PASS; R12 PASS.\n\n### Verified coverage and limits\n\nVerified r34-F1–F5’s textual repairs: AC39’s born-dead/cap assertions; AC43/R7’s conditional retirement completion; AC29/R9’s TTL pinning; AC1/R1’s per-surface literals. No implementation closure is claimed.\n\nByte domains, escaping, remaining-byte arithmetic and 1-based indices are specified. R1’s “no re-marshal” and R4’s `crypto/rand` provenance cannot be established from binary outputs alone.\n\nT1–T5 have named RED/GREEN checks; this revision contains no T6. C1–C4 prevent gating the affected tasks. Repairs require candidate-bound review; authorization remains absent.\n\n§7 uses existing infrastructure and bounded labels. Collector delivery remains unverified under the documented exception at `docs/logging-contract.md:619–630`; AC13/14 cover emission/scraping, not deployed delivery.\n\nNo silent resolution of a packet-marked owner decision found. OD1–OD3 remain explicit defaults, not owner approvals. Review was read-only; no checks were executed.\n\n### Verdict\nREVISE",
